{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact retained-floor draft2 eec4ec0eee7586f40bc037a5d93a9aff9956b7af/tree70dba9a9f85cd96accf948379f5b71f7a0a49501, primaryb15f902e1f658c0e1bd570f53bfd0de5f29380fe. Primary read FIRST IN FULL; direct governing promisebb6edf4a/requestf9fcee34 and assigned prepare binding verified. Six findings below together, based on exact unchanged source Main7a148325. These are static design counterexamples, not failures of an implemented floor or v2 reader.\n\n1. P1 — The kept set is not a deterministic function of recorded state as specified (sections3.6,4 rules3/7,6.7,7). Rule3 drops an old send once transport acknowledged it. Outbox.ts6-12 explicitly says that acknowledgement is bookkeeping and no journal entry; dispatch at179 calls SqliteStore.acknowledge, which only UPDATEs outbox.ack (sqlite.ts524-525). Two stores can therefore have the same journal/current folded state and identical FloorInput, with one acknowledgement present and the other absent. They compute different kept sets, counted entries and later read answers. Replay has no recorded acknowledgement basis from which to choose. LocalSigners' control cutoff/build progress is also storage bookkeeping, not a value in FloorInput; do not silently treat that phase-dependent anchor selection as a pure replay fold. Specify a canonical recorded basis for these retention decisions, or a conservative journal-derived kept rule independent of transport/index progress, with explicit trust/validation and native/replay agreement. Witness the same journal with acknowledgement timing differing and a restart; no entry needed by an unacknowledged send may be deleted. The repaired archive-receipt writer/judge/verifier split does not by itself solve this separate kept-set dependency.\n\n2. P1 — Preserve the sequence of a size proof, not only its smallest bound (note381-386). priorDetachedBounds(definition,context,at,before) at handlers.ts124-131 only considers seq\u003cbefore; the unchanged comment at handlers.ts153-159 explicitly rejects a later checkpoint deciding an earlier ambiguous binding. Suppose the kept bad-field deciding delivery is100 and the first successful size check for its same text digest is200. A floor deletes200 while100 remains kept for pending work. The proposed one-row/digest smallest-bound table now lets decisionBinding(100) use that later proof, where the original pre100 scan has no proof. Folding a factual size bound does not preserve this protocol's temporal evidence condition. Keep sufficient proof positions/bounds, or retain the exact earlier proof dependencies, so a query at deciding position n can only use a valid proof before n. Include a native/replay restart case where only a later proof exists, alongside the earlier-proof success case. The table's row domain, bounded storage/GC and reconstruction must preserve that query semantics.\n\n3. P2 — Define how an existing confirmed v1 archive enters v2 (sections2.2-2.4). Writer rule1 always starts after the last archive receipt; rule4 takes that commit/tree as parent and changes no other path. For a v1 confirmed receipt through99, the next v2 manifest begins100, but the v2 reader requires its first pass to start0. The old journal/v1 manifest/segments do not supply v2's missing0-99 pass index. Neither the fresh-v2 10k witness nor keeping the v1 reader unchanged covers this transition. Specify either a bounded exact conversion/seeding policy with pending/confirmed-row treatment and retained/S2b metadata preservation, or an explicit new binding/ref policy that starts a complete v2 prefix before a floor can rely on it. Witness continuation from a real confirmed v1 prefix and an interrupted pending v1 pass. No floor may trust an archive whose new reader cannot reconstruct its whole prefix.\n\n4. P2 — The 1,000-entry capacity witness cannot reach the first host floor under the written minimum (3.3 rule4,3.5 floor-recent,witness6 at458-462). Starting atF0, the host writes no floor until F\u003e=1000, and the judge needs at least100 entries at/above F. Thus at least1100 existing entries are needed before that first floor, but scopeEntries1000 stops the journal first (even before reserving the floor entry itself). Use test bounds/policy whose thresholds can be reached within admission, or explicitly define a trusted scaled test policy without weakening the production guard. State how room for the floor entry is admitted and add the boundary where reclamation is actually needed; the witness must show progress rather than bypass its own host rule.\n\n5. P2 — The flat Git directories contradict the claimed incremental work bound (2.2 paths;2.3 rule7 at111-113). A new journal/v2/passes/\u003cfirst\u003e.json changes one leaf, but its Git tree object still lists every old pass; the flat segments directory grows similarly. Building/verifying/rewriting that tree requires bytes/work proportional to the number of prior files, even though no old segment body is rewritten. For10000 previous small passes, the next single manifest requires a passes tree with10001 entries. The claim that pass cost is independent of archived history is therefore not supplied by this layout. Specify bounded fanout/sharding and charged tree/node/metadata work, or state and bound the cumulative tree cost rather than claiming only-new-entry work. Include an incremental witness with many already-confirmed small passes and actual tree-object work; leave existing v1 budgets/meaning separate from the new layout's explicit limits.\n\n6. P2 — Preserve the Chains membership-to-claim index across deletion/restart (6.6 at373-375), rather than treating archive coverage as a causal summary. signed-reads.ts275-292 discovers membership IDs from a register's delivery of a directory genesis and sets #claims from its resolved root. Reads.#open at123 requires claimOf(chained.membership.scope) for an authenticated membership session to read the register; that path deliberately has no signed-read time window. Once directory creation and acknowledgement finish, the discovery delivery can fall outside the seven kept rules even while the claim opening/current directory stays live. Walking kept entries plusF onward after restart no longer sees that delivery, so merely retaining the claim opening or an archive receipt does not reconstruct which membership it authorizes. A receipt contains neither that map nor root actor/time. Keep the needed derivation entries/evidence or define a deterministic bounded persisted index reconstructed in replay. Witness an established membership session reading its register after two floors/restart, plus an unrelated membership refusal; distinguish this from recent signed-entry eligibility and do not grant authority from archive coverage alone.\n\nDisposition of prior review1e274952: the original margin-before-step deadlock is fixed; full receipt confirmation is now honestly a writer check with clone verification, not mutable provider access in replay; lifetime actor/key bindings and unchanged accepted-set projection are restored; old-kept reclamation and retained-input/value byte charges are fixed; one cumulative current receipt plus bounded client catch-up addresses the multi-floor/old-clone gap. Fresh v2 tiling and same-pass S2b provenance are substantially specified. The remaining issues above are the new retention/replay details, v1 transition/cost and a contradictory capacity witness, not a request to restore the earlier proposals. Original limited-reader kept-input access is addressed, but temporal size proofs and Chains/index reconstruction still need the fixes described.\n\nNew source citations inspected against exact Git include archive-row17/49/134-140, driver212-217, prefix42, v1 manifest33-41/reader85-95, local-signers121-127, SQLite accepted/logical-byte projections, handlers124-131 and limited Reads139-143/186-191. Full source context37 files retained. No unsupported attribution of actual native failure or provider behavior. Minor prose correction: on20October, 'Room A passed4096 on21October' is a forecast and needs future/conditional tense. Whitespace diff check0; no project/compiler/native/browser tests or gate for this document-only change. No shared source/ref edit, merge, push, remote query, install or deployment. Exact-Git sources, new citation rows and six static counterexamples are in /private/tmp/checker-floor-d2-3baraz11, permanently frozen after CLOSED-MANIFEST. Complete review of any revised exact head remains required before implementation commissioning/landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b15f902e1f658c0e1bd570f53bfd0de5f29380fe","binding":"assigned","head":"eec4ec0eee7586f40bc037a5d93a9aff9956b7af","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:49d372bb7f005539aa8536f7f827fa50a6f5af76\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bb6edf4ab388a0a05bf5332890c734868be73acf\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5417d99d16c1ffe2d7897fdb761646bdf36e1ba5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}